<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # In Ten Years, I Will... – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Time Capsule</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> In Ten Years, I Will...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze the similarities and differences between two texts. Reading: Distinguish between facts and opinions in texts on everyday subjects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze similarities and differences in articles on skill development (e.g., "Both articles mention communication and critical thinking."). Can distinguish between factual information and opinions in two articles about future careers, with examples related to Panama (e.g., "The first article states that coding jobs are growing globally, while the second article argues that Panama needs to increase training for tech roles."). Can compare views in two texts about career paths for students (e.g., "I think this article about talking to family members about how they learned their skills is better advice than just reading about jobs on the internet.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #e9ecef;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images depicting various future-oriented professions and scenarios (e.g., a software developer, an engineer building something, a doctor, someone volunteering in a community project, a data analyst working remotely). Students are asked to briefly share which image resonates with them most and why, using phrases like "I might pursue..." or "I will be working as..." if they choose that career. This activates prior vocabulary from Lesson 1 like *career, technology, engineering, medicine, community service*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson, especially focusing on the long vowel /iː/ (e.g., "ach<b>ie</b>ve," "bel<b>ie</b>ve," "volunt<b>ee</b>r," "eng<b>i</b>n<b>ee</b>ring," "l<b>ea</b>dership") and stress in multisyllabic words or compounds (e.g., "prob-lem-SOLV-ing," "CRIT-i-cal THINK-ing," "com-MU-ni-ty SERV-ice," "high-de-MAND"). For instance, the teacher says: "In the future, I believe I will be achieving my goals through problem-solving."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of warmed-up vocabulary and concepts related to future planning.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you 'pursue' a career, are you starting it now or planning for the future?" (Expected: Planning for the future).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'high-demand' mean for a job? Are there many people working in it or many opportunities?" (Expected: Many opportunities, needs many workers).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Time Capsule".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Two short articles (approx. 200 words each) titled "Navigating Future Careers: Global Trends" (Article A) and "Panama's Future Workforce: Skills and Opportunities" (Article B). Both articles will discuss *career possibilities*, *skills* (like *critical thinking*, *problem-solving*, *leadership*), the importance of *education* and *training*, and mention different types of jobs (e.g., *software developer*, *data analyst*, *engineering*, *medicine*). They will include modals for possibilities ("students might consider...", "Panama could develop...") and some sentences using future continuous ("Many people will be working remotely...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and Interpreting Written Texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read both articles quickly and answer: "What is the main topic of Article A?" and "What is the main topic of Article B?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read specific statements and decide if they are True (T) or False (F) according to *both* texts. E.g., "According to both articles, communication skills are crucial for future *careers*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases or specific job roles to the article (A or B) where they are mentioned. E.g., "Focus on *technology* and digital *skills*." (A) vs. "Need for *community service* and local *opportunities*." (B).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Distinguishing facts vs. opinions, analyzing similarities and differences, using modals for career possibilities ("I might pursue..."), future continuous for ongoing plans ("I will be working as..."), and reported speech to share advice ("My teacher said that I should..."). Vocabulary includes *career, future, goals, possibilities, advice, education, training, technology, engineering, software developer, skills, leadership, problem-solving, critical thinking, community service, opportunities, pursue, accomplish, reflect, suggest, recommend, develop, research, enhance, explore, consider, gain, prepare, plan, train, learn, study, achieve, succeed, work, future-oriented, accomplished, skilled, practical, critical, adaptable, successful, educational, high-demand, relevant*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Fact vs. Opinion Challenge:</b> Students receive a worksheet with 8-10 sentences extracted from both articles. For each sentence, they must identify whether it is a Fact (F) or an Opinion (O). E.g., "Coding jobs are experiencing global growth." (F from Article A) vs. "Panama should prioritize *training* in *technology* above all other sectors." (O from Article B).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Transformation (Modals & Future Continuous):</b> Students complete sentences based on the texts, using appropriate modals of possibility (will, might, could) or the future continuous. E.g., "According to Article A, a *software developer* _________ (work) remotely in the future." (will be working/might be working). "The article *suggested that students* _________ (research) various *career possibilities*." (should research).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Advice Comparison (Reported Speech):</b> In pairs, students discuss the *advice* given in both articles. One student shares a piece of advice using *reported speech*, and the other responds with their opinion, comparing it to advice from the other article. E.g., "Article A *recommended that young people* develop *critical thinking* skills. But Article B *said that it's important to* also consider *community service*." Teacher reminds them to practice pronunciation of words with /iː/ and multisyllabic words like "leadership" and "problem-solving."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Plans and Time Capsule".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Article Analysis and Opinion Sharing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4) to collectively analyze the two articles. They are tasked with creating a short presentation (oral or a large poster) that:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Highlights 2-3 significant similarities between the articles (e.g., "Both articles mention communication and *critical thinking* as essential *skills*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explains 2-3 key differences in their perspectives or focus (e.g., "The first article states that *coding jobs* are growing globally, while the second article argues that Panama needs to increase *training* for *tech roles*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies at least two facts and two opinions from the articles, explaining why they classified them as such.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Concludes with the group's collective *advice* to their peers about preparing for future *careers*, integrating ideas from the articles and using modals for possibilities or future continuous. (e.g., "We believe students *might pursue* different *paths*, but they *will be developing* adaptable *skills*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use *reported speech* to reference specific points from the articles (e.g., "One article *suggested that we* explore *internships*."). They also organize class discussions about personal goals, assigning roles (recorder, presenter) within their groups, practicing the sociolinguistic competence.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in expressing analysis, distinguishing facts/opinions, comparing/contrasting, and collaborative interaction in formulating and presenting advice related to future plans.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and a brief checklist for group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their analysis and collective advice to the class. The teacher observes for active participation, analytical skills, and appropriate language use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially words with /iː/ and multisyllabic stress in vocabulary like *leadership, problem-solving, engineering*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (*career, goals, skills, high-demand, pursue, develop, advice, opportunities, future-oriented*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in distinguishing between facts and opinions in their analysis.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying and articulating similarities and differences between the two texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction within the group and effective communication during the presentation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modals for possibilities and future continuous in discussing future plans/advice.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two reflective prompts before leaving.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one *skill* you practiced today to *achieve* better reading comprehension, especially when comparing texts?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How easy or difficult was it to tell the difference between *facts* and *opinions* in the articles? Give one example of each."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn today about analyzing *future-oriented* texts and how different articles can provide diverse *advice*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will the *information* and *skills* from these articles help you think about your own *career possibilities* or what you *will be doing* in the future?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students distinguish facts from opinions and analyze similarities/differences? Was the language complexity appropriate for B1.1?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with the reading tasks or the vocabulary? Did all groups effectively embed the target grammar (modals, future continuous, reported speech)?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Today, students analyzed texts about future *career possibilities* and *advice*. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), they will use this information to actively discuss their personal *goals* and *future plans* for a time capsule with peers, focusing on expressing their own opinions and asking questions about others' aspirations, applying the *vocabulary* and *grammar* from both lessons in spoken interactions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>